course: english
lesson: 53
title: Writing assessment and the "Year 6 panic"
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In Year 6 lots of children get the "Year 6 panic": tummy aches, bad sleep and scary thoughts like "I'll fail and go to a rubbish secondary!"
explain: The truth is calmer: SATs mainly help show how well schools are teaching, and in most of England your secondary school place does not depend on your SATs results.
explain: Writing is not even a test on one day: your teacher looks at a collection of your work from across the year, so one bad piece does not ruin anything.
explain: Teachers check things like paragraphs, cohesion (ideas joined smoothly), punctuation and a range of sentence types: short and long, questions and exclamations.
explain: Picture Ruby crying at bedtime and her mum from Glasgow saying, "Wee lassie, it's no' the end o' the world" ("lassie" means girl): talking about the worry, sleeping, eating well and taking breaks help far more than worrying harder.
fact: Year 6 writing in England is assessed by the teacher from work across the year; there is no single writing exam.
fact: Writing is judged on things like paragraphs, cohesion, punctuation, spelling and a range of sentence types.
fact: Cohesion means a piece holds together: words like "however", "after that" and "as a result" link the ideas.
fact: SATs results mainly help show how schools are doing; in most of England, secondary school places do not depend on them.
fact: Some areas have grammar schools with their own separate entrance test, the 11-plus, which is not the SATs.
fact: Feeling worried before tests is very common; many classmates feel the same even if they look fine.
fact: Stress can spoil sleep and focus, so rest, food, breaks and talking it over help.
fact: Worrying more does not make revision work better.
fact: Revision games and quizzes are real practice, and they make it less stressful.
fact: SATs began in England in the 1990s; before that schools had other tests.
fact: Celebrating everyone's effort after SATs is good, because results are not the only thing that counts.
fact: Slang to know: in Liverpool "proper" means really and "our kid" means my brother or sister; Gen Alpha "it's giving doom" means "it feels like doom"; in Newcastle "divvent" means don't and "canny" means quite or nice.
exercise: How is Year 6 writing assessed in England? | By the teacher, from work across the year | By one long, timed writing exam in May at school | By a computer marking a single story | By the head reading one page of it
exercise: Ruby sobs, "I'm gonna fail SATs and go to a rubbish secondary!" What is true? | In most areas, places don't depend on SATs | She's right: SATs pick her secondary school | Failing SATs means staying in Year 6 again | Only children with top marks get a place
exercise: What do SATs mainly help to show? | How well schools help children progress | Which children will be clever forever | Who gets into which secondary school | Who is allowed to join the sports teams
exercise: A mum in Glasgow says, "Wee lassie, it's no' the end o' the world." What does "lassie" mean? | Girl | Dog | Teacher | Silly thing
exercise: "Use a range of sentence types." Which set shows a range? | A question, a short sentence and a long one | Three short sentences, one after another | Three sentences that all begin with "Then" | One very, very long sentence with no end
exercise: What is cohesion in writing? | Ideas linked so the piece holds together | Writing as many pages as you possibly can | Writing every single word in capitals | Using only short words in every sentence
exercise: Which word helps cohesion? | However | Banana | Very | Pineapple
exercise: Kai says, "I'm proper stressed, our kid." What helps with stress? | Breaks, sleep and talking about it | Staying up all night revising, every night | Keeping the worry a total secret | Skipping meals to study more
exercise: Grace says, "SATs are giving doom." What does she mean? | SATs feel like doom | SATs give out prizes | SATs are cancelled this year | SATs are very easy to pass
exercise: Is everyone else in the class really fine? | Many feel the same, even if they hide it | Yes, you are the only one who worries | Yes, worrying is only for little babies | No, they are all far more worried than you
exercise: Does worrying more help revision? | No; rest and calm help more | Yes, the more worry, the better | Yes, but only late at night in bed | No, but crying before bed does
exercise: The teacher says, "Revision games today." Is a game real revision? | Yes, quizzes and games are real practice | No, revision only works if it's boring | No, games are only for after the SATs | Yes, but only for maths lessons, not for English
exercise: Dad says, "In my day, we didn't have SATs." When did SATs start in England? | In the 1990s | Last year | In Victorian times | In the 1500s
exercise: Did schools have tests before SATs? | Yes, other tests | No, no tests at all | Only teachers did | Only spelling bees
exercise: One of Ruby's stories goes badly. What happens? | Not much; the teacher looks at many pieces | She fails Year 6 writing, there and then | She must do the whole of Year 6 again | Her secondary school place is taken away
exercise: What is a good thing to do after SATs? | Celebrate everyone's effort | Praise only the top scorers | Never write again, ever | Start revising for next year
test: Your mate says, "Mess up SATs and you're stuck in Year 6 forever, bruv." ("Bruv" means mate.) Put him right. | Everyone moves up; SATs don't hold you back | He's right, you would have to redo the year | Only if you fail two of the papers | Only if your writing is the worst in class
test: Tia, Gen Alpha: "Me and my bestie are delulu, we reckon we'll get zero." ("Delulu" means believing something unrealistic.) What would help them? | Talking to a teacher about what's realistic | Keeping it secret and worrying even more | Giving up revising, since it is hopeless | Revising all night, every single night
test: What would probably happen if Ruby kept her worry secret every night? | It would grow and spoil her sleep | It would vanish on its own at once | She would do much better because of it | Her teacher would surely guess it anyway
test: A cartoon says one writing test on one day decides your Year 6 writing. Fix it. | It's teacher-assessed from the whole year | The cartoon is right about the writing | There is no writing at all in Year 6 | Writing is marked by a robot in London
test: Uncle in Newcastle: "Divvent fret, bonny lad, it's canny easy." What is he saying? | Don't worry, it's quite easy | Don't eat it, it's very hard to chew | Don't go there, it is too scary | Don't try, there's no point at all
test: Which piece has the best cohesion? | First we packed. After that, we drove. As a result, we arrived early. | We packed. We drove. We arrived early. We had packed. We had driven. | We drove. First we packed. Arrived. Then we packed again, I think. | Packed drove arrived early we, and then after all of that came the packing.
test: Your big sister snaps, "Shut up about SATs, I'm sick of it." What is most likely going on? | She is stressed and needs a break | She hates you forever now | She has already failed them | She is perfectly happy and calm
test: Dad, dry: "In my day, we didn't have SATs." Child: "Lucky." What is true? | Dad's school still had other tests | Nobody had any tests back then | SATs are hundreds of years old | Dad is lying about his school days
test: Which helps most the night before SATs? | A good meal, a calm evening and sleep | Revising until three in the morning | Watching a really scary film | Drinking lots and lots of fizzy pop at bedtime
test: Which of these is a grammar school entrance test, not a SATs paper? | The 11-plus | The reading paper | The spelling test | The maths paper
test: Ruby wrote 30 pieces this year and 3 went badly. How many went well? | 27 | 3 | 33 | 30
exam: Identify the manner in which Year 6 writing is assessed in England. | Teacher assessment of work across the year | A single timed examination in May | Computer marking of one composition | External assessment of one paragraph
exam: Select the response most consistent with the established facts concerning admission to secondary school in most of England. | It does not ordinarily depend upon SATs | It depends wholly upon SATs results | It is determined by the writing alone | It is reserved for those with full marks
exam: Ascertain the term denoting the linking of ideas such that a written piece holds together. | Cohesion | Projection | Alliteration | Rebuttal
exam: It is given that a pupil experiences anxiety prior to examinations. Identify the most appropriate response. | Discuss it with family or a teacher and rest | Conceal it and revise throughout the night | Abandon all revision entirely | Regard it as proof of certain failure
